Here is a survey of the existing patterns in this codebase plus the Next.js docs that ship with the installed version, so you can implement `UserDashboard` consistently.

## 1. Where components are typically located

The project uses the **App Router** and keeps components inside `app/`:

| Path | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `/workspace/app/layout.tsx` | Root layout |
| `/workspace/app/page.tsx` | Home page (already fetches data) |
| `/workspace/app/UserDashboard.tsx` | Existing stub for your component |

Relevant configuration for imports:

`/workspace/tsconfig.json`
```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./*"]
    }
  }
}
```

So you can import with `@/app/UserDashboard` or `@/components/...`, but the current code uses relative imports (`./UserDashboard`).

The Next.js project-structure docs note that you can also colocate components in private folders like `app/_components/` or `app/_lib/` to keep them from becoming routes.

## 2. Existing async server component example

`/workspace/app/page.tsx` is the only existing async data-fetching component:

```tsx
import UserDashboard from './UserDashboard'

// Example of App Router data fetching
async function getStaticData() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/stats')
    return res.json()
  } catch {
    // Return mock data for build time
    return { users: 100 }
  }
}

export default async function Page() {
  const stats = await getStaticData()

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Dashboard</h1>
      <p>Total users: {stats.users}</p>
      <UserDashboard />
    </div>
  )
}
```

And the current stub for your component:

`/workspace/app/UserDashboard.tsx`
```tsx
export default function UserDashboard() {
  // TODO: Implement user dashboard that needs to fetch user-specific data on each request
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>User Dashboard</h2>
      <p>User dashboard content goes here</p>
    </div>
  )
}
```

## 3. Conventions for data fetching, loading, and errors

### Async server component fetch pattern

From the installed docs:

`/workspace/node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/01-getting-started/06-fetching-data.md`
```tsx
export default async function Page() {
  const data = await fetch('https://api.vercel.app/blog')
  const posts = await data.json()
  return (
    <ul>
      {posts.map((post) => (
        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}
```

### Fetching fresh data on every request

Because you need `https://api.example.com/user/preferences` on **each request**, use `cache: 'no-store'`. From the installed `fetch` docs:

`/workspace/node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/03-api-reference/04-functions/fetch.md`
```ts
fetch(`https://...`, { cache: 'no-store' })
```

> `no-store`: Next.js fetches the resource from the remote server on every request.

### Streaming / loading states

For data that must run at request time, the installed caching docs recommend wrapping the async component in `<Suspense>` rather than blocking the whole route:

`/workspace/node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/01-getting-started/08-caching.md`
```tsx
import { Suspense } from 'react'

async function LatestPosts() {
  const data = await fetch('https://api.example.com/posts')
  const posts = await data.json()
  return (
    <ul>
      {posts.map((post) => (
        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}

export default function Page() {
  return (
    <>
      <h1>My Blog</h1>
      <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading posts...</p>}>
        <LatestPosts />
      </Suspense>
    </>
  )
}
```

You can also use the `loading.tsx` file convention:

`/workspace/node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/03-api-reference/03-file-conventions/loading.md`
```tsx
// app/dashboard/loading.tsx
export default function Loading() {
  return <LoadingSkeleton />
}
```

### Error handling

For expected fetch failures, handle them in the Server Component:

`/workspace/node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/01-getting-started/10-error-handling.md`
```tsx
export default async function Page() {
  const res = await fetch(`https://...`)
  const data = await res.json()

  if (!res.ok) {
    return 'There was an error.'
  }

  return '...'
}
```

For unexpected runtime errors, add a route-level error boundary. Error boundaries **must** be Client Components:

`/workspace/node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/03-api-reference/03-file-conventions/error.md`
```tsx
'use client' // Error boundaries must be Client Components

import { useEffect } from 'react'

export default function Error({
  error,
  retry,
}: {
  error: Error & { digest?: string }
  retry: () => void
}) {
  useEffect(() => {
    console.error(error)
  }, [error])

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Something went wrong!</h2>
      <button onClick={() => retry()}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

## 4. Relevant configuration files

- `/workspace/next.config.ts` — currently empty; `cacheComponents` is **not** enabled, so the legacy fetch caching model applies.
- `/workspace/tsconfig.json` — sets `@/*` path alias to `./*`.
- `/workspace/package.json` — uses `next@^16.3.0-canary.50`, `react@19.1.0`, TypeScript, and Vitest.

## 5. Recommended pattern for `UserDashboard`

Based on the existing code and the installed Next.js 16 docs, here is the pattern to follow:

```tsx
// /workspace/app/UserDashboard.tsx
export default async function UserDashboard() {
  const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/user/preferences', {
    cache: 'no-store',
  })

  if (!res.ok) {
    return <p>Unable to load preferences.</p>
  }

  const preferences = await res.json()

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>User Dashboard</h2>
      <pre>{JSON.stringify(preferences, null, 2)}</pre>
    </div>
  )
}
```

Then wrap it in a `<Suspense>` boundary where it is used so it streams in without blocking the rest of the page:

```tsx
// /workspace/app/page.tsx
import { Suspense } from 'react'
import UserDashboard from './UserDashboard'

async function getStaticData() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/stats')
    return res.json()
  } catch {
    return { users: 100 }
  }
}

export default async function Page() {
  const stats = await getStaticData()

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Dashboard</h1>
      <p>Total users: {stats.users}</p>
      <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading user preferences...</p>}>
        <UserDashboard />
      </Suspense>
    </div>
  )
}
```

If you want route-level error recovery, also add:

```tsx
// /workspace/app/error.tsx
'use client'

import { useEffect } from 'react'

export default function Error({
  error,
  retry,
}: {
  error: Error & { digest?: string }
  retry: () => void
}) {
  useEffect(() => {
    console.error(error)
  }, [error])

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Something went wrong loading the dashboard.</h2>
      <button onClick={() => retry()}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

Key takeaways:
- Make `UserDashboard` an `async` Server Component.
- Use `fetch(url, { cache: 'no-store' })` for per-request freshness.
- Wrap it in `<Suspense>` so it streams without blocking the rest of the page.
- Handle expected errors inline; use `error.tsx` for unexpected errors.

